--- body ---
## Context
Observed in sen-causal-reading on padme, 2026-11-09. After `/clear`, the
runtime started a new session id (transcripts: `b33fb9a4-…` before, `53df30ba-…`
after). `/clear` fires SessionEnd for the old id, and `scripts/on-end.sh`
removes `<temp-root>/<cwd-key>/<old-id>/` unconditionally. The new session's
system prompt still advertised `…/b33fb9a4-…/scratchpad` as its scratchpad, so
the first write to it failed (`No such file or directory`); the runtime later
withdrew the path. The new session's own directory existed and worked.

Cause, established from the transcripts and the hook source: the hook treats
`/clear` like an exit; the runtime advertises the previous id's scratchpad
across `/clear`. The second half is runtime behaviour outside the harness.

Author's view (2026-11-09): a runtime command such as `/clear` should not
delete a directory.

## Proposed fix
`on-end.sh` reads `reason` from the SessionEnd payload and skips removal when
it is `clear`; `session_scratch.py --sweep` (molt step 1.7) reclaims the
directory once no live process holds it.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `on-end.sh` keeps the directory when the payload's `reason` is `clear`,
      and removes it for other reasons as today
- [ ] Test covering both payloads
- [ ] The sweep still reclaims a cleared session's directory once orphaned
